    <h1>Cosmetic peptide raw materials: the formulation buyer's guide</h1>
    <div class="quick-answer"><strong>Quick answer.</strong> A cosmetic peptide raw material should be bought on identity, purity, counter-ion, water content, colour/appearance and microbial status — not on the word "cosmetic grade". For copper peptides, colour is part of the specification; for glutathione, oxidation state is.</div>
    <p class="lead muted">"Cosmetic grade" is the least defined grade in this market. It can mean anything from "not pharmaceutical" to "we did not test it". This framework treats cosmetic peptides as what they are: chemical raw materials with a specification, a test panel and a stability profile.</p>

    <div class="note"><strong>Scope.</strong> Raw-material sourcing and formulation research for cosmetic ingredients. This guide makes no efficacy, anti-ageing or dermatological claims; finished-product safety and claims are the formulator's responsibility.</div>

    <h2>Why "cosmetic grade" is a weak specification</h2>
    <p>Pharmaceutical grades are anchored to pharmacopoeial monographs and regulatory expectations. Cosmetic grades are not. In practice, the label often means only that the material is intended for cosmetic use — not that a defined panel was tested. Buyers who accept the label as a specification inherit whatever the supplier decided to test.</p>
    <p><strong>Our position:</strong> replace the grade label with a test list. Identity, purity, counter-ion, water, appearance and microbial status are the minimum; for copper peptides and glutathione, add the material-specific attributes below.</p>

    <h2>The three material families</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Chemical character</th><th>What makes it analytically distinct</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>GHK-Cu</td><td>Copper tripeptide complex</td><td>Blue colour, copper content, pH-dependent speciation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AHK-Cu</td><td>Copper tripeptide complex</td><td>Different sequence, copper binding and colour behaviour</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Glutathione</td><td>Reduced tripeptide (GSH)</td><td>Oxidation to GSSG, sulfhydryl reactivity, colourless</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Other cosmetic peptides (SNAP-8, Matrixyl-type sequences) follow the same general logic: identity, purity, counter-ion, water and microbial status — with sequence-specific handling.</p>

    <h2>The analytical panel</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Why a formulator needs it</th><th>Typical method</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Identity</td><td>Confirms the sequence/complex you ordered</td><td>ESI-MS / MALDI-TOF</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Purity</td><td>Impurity load affects colour, stability and formulation</td><td>RP-HPLC with chromatogram and method</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Counter-ion</td><td>Acetate/TFA changes mass, pH and compatibility</td><td>Ion chromatography or supplier statement</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water content</td><td>Affects weighing, stability and microbial risk</td><td>Karl Fischer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Appearance / colour</td><td>For copper peptides, colour is a product attribute</td><td>Visual against a defined standard</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Microbial status</td><td>Raw material bioburden matters for preservative strategy</td><td>Total aerobic count / specified method</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Copper content (Cu peptides)</td><td>Confirms the complex, not just the peptide</td><td>Atomic absorption / ICP</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Colour is data (copper peptides)</h2>
    <p>GHK-Cu and AHK-Cu are blue because of the copper–peptide complex. Colour intensity and hue are not cosmetic trivia: they are a visible signal of the complex's state. A batch that is pale, grey or greenish is asking a question that a visual check should answer before formulation.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Define an acceptance range for colour against a retained reference sample.</li>
      <li>Ask for copper content, not only peptide purity.</li>
      <li>Record colour at receipt and after storage; drift is an early stability signal.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Oxidation state is data (glutathione)</h2>
    <p>Glutathione is supplied as the reduced form (GSH), which oxidises to the disulfide (GSSG) over time and with exposure to oxygen, light and trace metals. A purity figure that does not distinguish GSH from GSSG is incomplete for formulation purposes.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ask for the assay of reduced glutathione and, where available, the GSSG content.</li>
      <li>Store sealed, dry, protected from light and oxygen; avoid metal contact.</li>
      <li>Expect colour and odour changes as early indicators of oxidation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>pH, counter-ion and compatibility</h2>
    <p>Copper peptides are pH-sensitive: speciation and colour change with pH, and chelating or reducing ingredients can strip or alter the copper. Glutathione is a reducing agent and reacts with oxidants and some metal ions. Neither behaviour is a defect — it is chemistry the formulation must accommodate.</p>
    <p><strong>Our rule:</strong> request the counter-ion and the recommended pH window in writing, and run a compatibility check before committing to a formula. A raw material that is 98% pure can still be incompatible with your system.</p>

    <h2>Stability and packaging</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Light:</strong> amber or opaque containers; copper complexes and glutathione are both light-sensitive.</li>
      <li><strong>Oxygen:</strong> nitrogen headspace or minimal headspace for glutathione; sealed barriers for both.</li>
      <li><strong>Moisture:</strong> desiccant and sealed primary packaging.</li>
      <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> cool storage; avoid freeze–thaw of solutions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/knowledge/cosmetic-peptide-stability-packaging/">Stability and packaging for cosmetic peptides</a> covers the detail.</p>

    <h2>Sourcing verification</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Pass condition</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Identity</td><td>MS consistent with the declared sequence/complex</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Purity</td><td>HPLC chromatogram with method and integration</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Counter-ion</td><td>Stated and consistent with the MS interpretation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water</td><td>Karl Fischer value reported</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Appearance</td><td>Colour within the agreed range (reference retained)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Microbial</td><td>Method and result stated where claimed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Batch linkage</td><td>Lot number on vial, COA and invoice</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><a href="/knowledge/cosmetic-peptide-sourcing-checklist/">Cosmetic peptide sourcing checklist</a> turns this into a working document.</p>

    <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
    <p><strong>Position.</strong> Cosmetic peptide buyers should demand a pharmaceutical-style analytical panel minus the clinical claims. The category's quality problem is not chemistry — it is that nobody defined the specification, so suppliers optimise for price and appearance.</p>
    <p><strong>Reasoning.</strong> A formulator cannot compensate for an unidentified counter-ion or an oxidised raw material. The cost of a batch that fails compatibility is much larger than the price difference between a documented and an undocumented supplier.</p>
    <p><strong>Disclosure.</strong> We supply cosmetic peptide raw materials and benefit when buyers prefer documented lots. We also decline to describe these materials as having cosmetic effects — that is a formulation and claims decision, not a supplier's.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
    <h3>Is "cosmetic grade" a regulated grade?</h3>
    <p>No single international definition exists. Treat it as an intended-use statement and replace it with a written test panel.</p>
    <h3>Why is my GHK-Cu a different shade from the last batch?</h3>
    <p>Colour depends on the copper complex, concentration and moisture. A shift outside your agreed range is a reason to test copper content and purity, not to assume it is cosmetic variation.</p>
    <h3>Can glutathione be used in a formula with oxidising actives?</h3>
    <p>It is a reducing agent and reacts with oxidants. Compatibility testing is required; a supplier cannot answer this for your formula.</p>
    <h3>Do you provide microbial testing?</h3>
    <p>On request, with the method stated. If a supplier claims microbial status without a method and result, treat the claim as unverified.</p>

    <h2>Sources &amp; evidence</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Analytical methods:</strong> ICH Q2(R1) — <a href="https://www.ich.org/page/quality-guidelines" rel="noopener" target="_blank">ICH quality guidelines</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Water content:</strong> USP &lt;731&gt; — <a href="https://www.usp.org/chemical-medicines" rel="noopener" target="_blank">USP</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Chinese Pharmacopoeia (ChP 2020), Part IV:</strong> General Chapter 0512 (HPLC), 0832 (loss on drying) — cited by chapter number.</li>
      <li><strong>Reference structures:</strong> PubChem entries for GHK-Cu and glutathione; batch COA governs the material supplied.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small muted">No laboratory testing was performed by the author for this guide; acceptance limits must come from your own specification.</p>

